  "editorial": {
    "status": "draft"
  },
  "sourceRefs": [
    {
      "sourceId": "source:heydemann-ostrogothic-kingdom",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Discussion after note 99 and before note 100. The political union with the Visigothic kingdom dissolved on Athalaric’s accession, and Amalaric became king there."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:heydemann-ostrogothic-kingdom",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Introduction, opening paragraphs through note 4. Odoacer in 476, the eastern emperor Zeno’s mandate, the Italian campaign in 489, the agreement and killing in 493, and Theoderic’s death in 526."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:anonymus-valesianus-theoderic",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Pars posterior 49–51. The imperial commission, the Isonzo and Odoacer’s camp on a plain outside Verona before the battle."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:anonymus-valesianus-theoderic",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Pars posterior 53–56. Camp in the Pineta, reported price of a measure of wheat, difficulties during the siege, entry into Ravenna and Odoacer’s death."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:venning-western-europe-2018",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Part 1, pp. 19–21, entries for Italy under 489, 490, 492 and 493. Verona, the siege of Ravenna and the additional naval blockade from 492."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:anonymus-valesianus-theoderic",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Pars posterior 65–69. Reception by Pope Symmachus and the Senate, distribution of grain and annual funds earmarked from the wine tax."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:venning-western-europe-2018",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Part 1, pp. 23–24. Entry for 500 under Italy on the visit to Rome."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:guerrini-theoderic-epigraphy",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "pp. 138–139, fig. 3, notes 28–31 and catalogue no. 5. Location at Via di Roma and Via Guaccimanni, lead pipe stamps and Cassiodorus, Chronica, entry for 502."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:anonymus-valesianus-theoderic",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Pars posterior 71. The author credits Theoderic with restoring the aqueduct at Ravenna attributed to Trajan."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:cassiodorus-variae-5-38",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "V.38, heading and §§1–2. Landholders are ordered to remove saplings by the roots from Ravenna’s aqueduct. The letter discusses drinking and bathing water."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:unesco-early-christian-ravenna",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Outstanding Universal Value, Brief synthesis, paragraphs on the Arian Baptistery, Sant’Apollinare Nuovo, the mausoleum and the Archbishop’s Chapel."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:whelan-people-in-the-palace",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "pp. 242–244, figs. 7.1–7.2 and notes 1–6. The palace facade, remnants of hands after the redesign, and readings of the gesture as a greeting or prayer."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:heydemann-ostrogothic-kingdom",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Paragraphs around notes 84–88. Athalaric, his minority and his mother Amalasuentha’s regency, the accession letters and the promised continuation of rights."
    },
    {
      "sourceId": "source:heydemann-ostrogothic-kingdom",
      "role": "supports",
      "locator": "Discussion of Witigis and Belisarius, paragraphs around notes 113–120. Surrender of Ravenna in 540 and its continued imperial control during the remaining war."
    }
  ],
